---
title: "Performance notes"
description: "The two habits that matter and the one measurement that settles the rest."
---

## Filter early, collect once {#sec-perf-filter}

The planner pushes filters toward the readers, so writing them late costs nothing —
but only when the filter is expressible as a predicate over columns. A filter that
calls into Python per row cannot be pushed and forces the whole frame into memory
first. Keep predicates in `col()` expressions and the pushdown holds.

Collecting more than once re-runs the whole plan. If two results come from a shared
prefix, collect the prefix once and branch from the materialised frame.

## Measuring instead of guessing {#sec-perf-explain}

`explain()` prints the plan Tarn will actually run, after pushdown and pruning:

```python
print(sales.filter(tarn.col("region") == "north").explain())
```

Read it bottom-up. If a filter you wrote appears above the reader rather than inside
it, the pushdown did not happen, and that is the thing to fix before anything else.
